<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Algorithms on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/tags/algorithms/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/tags/algorithms/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Free Algebras: Why Lists and Polynomials Are Universal</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/</guid><description>The free monoid on a set is the type of lists over that set. The universal property says fold is the unique homomorphism from lists to any monoid. This explains why lists, multisets, and polynomials appear everywhere.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algorithms</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>category-theory</category></item><item><title>Homomorphisms: The Maps Between Structures</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/</guid><description>A homomorphism preserves structure. fold is the universal homomorphism from the free monoid. This is the algebraic reason that fold, evaluation, and parallelism work.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algorithms</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>homomorphisms</category></item><item><title>Lattices: Fixed Points and Iteration</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-lattice-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-lattice-stepanov/</guid><description>A lattice has two operations, meet and join, satisfying absorption laws. Tarski&amp;#39;s theorem gives a generic fixed-point algorithm. Lattice structure determines the iteration, just as monoid structure determines power-by-squaring.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-lattice-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algorithms</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>lattices</category><category>abstract-interpretation</category></item><item><title>Semirings: One Algorithm, Six Graph Problems</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-semiring-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-semiring-stepanov/</guid><description>A semiring has two monoidal operations linked by distributivity. Matrix multiplication over different semirings gives shortest paths, longest paths, widest paths, reachability, and path counting, all from the same code.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-semiring-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algorithms</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>semirings</category><category>graph-theory</category></item><item><title>Streaming Statistics, One Monoid at a Time</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/</guid><description>Online accumulators are monoids. Default construction is the identity, combination via += is the binary operation, and parallel composition gives the product monoid, computing arbitrary statistics in a single pass.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algorithms</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>statistics</category></item><item><title>Seeing Structure First</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Sun, 18 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/</guid><description>A reflection on eleven explorations in generic programming, and how algorithms arise from algebraic structure.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algorithms</media:title></me